[{:db/id "q-332655/field-1/9b7914b1b710e5261a78276d259d6d5990c75f13add52397e593f16fd83cd321" :answer/id #uuid "6a3762d3-72e9-5bdc-b420-a9e8c7b1a61e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}e^{-x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332655/field-1/c5ef0395c08b7b9e6b06b02c5626c30efa64ea2ac9cacbc8f9e0695e54e630cd" :answer/id #uuid "91bb20db-67c9-5578-88f9-2bbb1ebec1ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}e^{-x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332655/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "761fadef-426b-5d9d-b1c2-c1d1dd9bb1b5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332655/field-1/9b7914b1b710e5261a78276d259d6d5990c75f13add52397e593f16fd83cd321" :answer-field/choices ["q-332655/field-1/9b7914b1b710e5261a78276d259d6d5990c75f13add52397e593f16fd83cd321" "q-332655/field-1/c5ef0395c08b7b9e6b06b02c5626c30efa64ea2ac9cacbc8f9e0695e54e630cd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332655" :question/id #uuid "665d1b75-cdd7-52b1-bb0c-78810441bf38" :question/math-academy-id "q-332655" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-20y=15{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{4x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $15{e}^{-x},$ and ${e}^{-x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha {e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-\\alpha {e}^{-x})-20(\\alpha {e}^{-x}) & =15{e}^{-x} \\\\ (-5-20)\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =15{e}^{-x} \\\\ -25\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =15{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{5}{e}^{-x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332655/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bcc7e2-88f7-5ef9-8c51-77c1e2c8e8af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332655"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332668/field-1/677fedcb7ada2980240a7c168360e511e3c3dd6c0bbccb00a147f78a9fbe9309" :answer/id #uuid "8e63b12a-6ceb-51f9-a630-adac55902e3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}e^x+\\frac{4}{21}e^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332668/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "39a18d23-5024-5935-8c97-6b1c447792ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332668/field-1/677fedcb7ada2980240a7c168360e511e3c3dd6c0bbccb00a147f78a9fbe9309" :answer-field/choices ["q-332668/field-1/677fedcb7ada2980240a7c168360e511e3c3dd6c0bbccb00a147f78a9fbe9309"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332668" :question/id #uuid "32b00adf-08fa-5ac3-9680-3e510439c0f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-332668" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+9y=7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x},$ and neither ${e}^{x}$ nor ${e}^{4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+4\\beta {e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(\\alpha {e}^{x}+4\\beta {e}^{4x})+9(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{4x}) & =7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x} \\\\ (3\\alpha +9\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(12\\beta +9\\beta ){e}^{4x} & =7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x} \\\\ 12\\alpha {e}^{x}+21\\beta {e}^{4x} & =7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{x}$ and ${e}^{4x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n12\\alpha =7\\,\\text{and}\\,21\\beta =4,\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{7}{12}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =\\frac{4}{21}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{7}{12}{e}^{x}+\\frac{4}{21}{e}^{4x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332668/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bcc7e2-88f7-5ef9-8c51-77c1e2c8e8af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332668"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332667/field-1/1bbff932e4d6ca51ba86fbc43467605c960ba98f5d592b47c913a79a38439129" :answer/id #uuid "6bf6e6d9-6f37-546e-834b-b894447829ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16}e^{2x}+\\frac{3}{20}e^{3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332667/field-1/ba2411b10fe7a77240900d5b9772094f8529d4fe4e9d391c515fb49d7ba38793" :answer/id #uuid "7fe06c78-5546-5468-bc5e-83eb9742e5e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16}e^{2x}-\\frac{3}{20}e^{3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332667/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d51d996a-1593-5719-98be-7e17daf8bd96" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332667/field-1/1bbff932e4d6ca51ba86fbc43467605c960ba98f5d592b47c913a79a38439129" :answer-field/choices ["q-332667/field-1/1bbff932e4d6ca51ba86fbc43467605c960ba98f5d592b47c913a79a38439129" "q-332667/field-1/ba2411b10fe7a77240900d5b9772094f8529d4fe4e9d391c515fb49d7ba38793"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332667" :question/id #uuid "2217fd02-a90b-5b30-9964-3afd9105a48a" :question/math-academy-id "q-332667" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=5{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x},$ and neither ${e}^{2x}$ nor ${e}^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+3\\beta {e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+3\\beta {e}^{3x})+8(\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{3x}) & =5{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x} \\\\ (8\\alpha +8\\alpha ){e}^{2x}+(12\\beta +8\\beta ){e}^{3x} & =5{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x} \\\\ 16\\alpha {e}^{2x}+20\\beta {e}^{3x} & =5{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{2x}$ and ${e}^{3x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n16\\alpha =5,\\,\\,20\\beta =3.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{5}{16},\\,\\,\\beta =\\frac{3}{20}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{5}{16}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{3}{20}{e}^{3x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-332667/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bcc7e2-88f7-5ef9-8c51-77c1e2c8e8af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332667"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332664/field-1/89939416e86af27a29df0fe0c1e092b12510a681ba9fdec636c67b16e3ff8a1f" :answer/id #uuid "c5053c51-a1ba-53a5-b1d0-f8c8f3ac2b2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-e^{-5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332664/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "057d35da-466f-5716-ae3a-677ffe8caebe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332664/field-1/89939416e86af27a29df0fe0c1e092b12510a681ba9fdec636c67b16e3ff8a1f" :answer-field/choices ["q-332664/field-1/89939416e86af27a29df0fe0c1e092b12510a681ba9fdec636c67b16e3ff8a1f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332664" :question/id #uuid "80217b49-aac6-571e-ac98-6ae5f97efe41" :question/math-academy-id "q-332664" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+14y=21{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $21{e}^{-5x},$ and ${e}^{-5x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-5\\alpha {e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(-5\\alpha {e}^{-5x})+14(\\alpha {e}^{-5x}) & =21{e}^{-5x} \\\\ (-35+14)\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =21{e}^{-5x} \\\\ -21\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =21{e}^{-5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{e}^{-5x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332664/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bcc7e2-88f7-5ef9-8c51-77c1e2c8e8af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332664"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332670/field-1/c00193e1c5a8559f3d732e7bd5fb84cc39a25744fb10bd07f489533917ea407a" :answer/id #uuid "ded373d6-e3a7-5ae2-bff6-4cb4e46263ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}e^x+e^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332670/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "72563d45-b8a2-5c79-ac74-8ffbb1b1368b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332670/field-1/c00193e1c5a8559f3d732e7bd5fb84cc39a25744fb10bd07f489533917ea407a" :answer-field/choices ["q-332670/field-1/c00193e1c5a8559f3d732e7bd5fb84cc39a25744fb10bd07f489533917ea407a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332670" :question/id #uuid "2ed16f1e-5ea8-5e61-9dde-90ceef762829" :question/math-academy-id "q-332670" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x},$ and neither ${e}^{x}$ nor ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\alpha {e}^{x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x})-6(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{2x}) & =3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x} \\\\ (2\\alpha -6\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(4\\beta -6\\beta ){e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x} \\\\ -4\\alpha {e}^{x}-2\\beta {e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{x}$ and ${e}^{2x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha =3,\\,\\,-2\\beta =-2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\,\\beta =1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332670/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bcc7e2-88f7-5ef9-8c51-77c1e2c8e8af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332670"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332728/field-1/2dd145634d5b4fcd6d99f8608abf9f2f545a61864ea3413937883d5ae132d10d" :answer/id #uuid "0eac0720-d094-5c11-a613-f13f3aea6fdf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8xe^{5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332728/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "774e0589-6fc8-53b4-90b8-7d9ab66d7716" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332728/field-1/2dd145634d5b4fcd6d99f8608abf9f2f545a61864ea3413937883d5ae132d10d" :answer-field/choices ["q-332728/field-1/2dd145634d5b4fcd6d99f8608abf9f2f545a61864ea3413937883d5ae132d10d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332728" :question/id #uuid "628da0cc-0f9a-5282-9f1d-c8b6b2f1d84a" :question/math-academy-id "q-332728" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=8{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{5x}+5x{e}^{5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x})-5\\alpha x{e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ (1+5x-5x)\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{5x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332728/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c7ce128-42e5-5a5a-8fa2-0e897504ff9d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332728"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332853/field-1/b4b837c694ca5d8be9308df49d2a8650419e14179be4c20143aec7221e664f88" :answer/id #uuid "cb9ddf08-4d90-53e0-99a1-118bd7f9ab2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30xe^{-6x}+\\frac{4}{7}e^x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332853/field-1/517cdf3eae1bd4e0b288721ae6619a788a0f80d4dcce78a511cf52a77482270f" :answer/id #uuid "8249cb59-8474-524c-8206-9882b70ebebf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30xe^{-6x}-\\frac{4}{7}e^x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332853/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a1ca33a2-5f2d-5692-bcf6-db46389868b5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332853/field-1/b4b837c694ca5d8be9308df49d2a8650419e14179be4c20143aec7221e664f88" :answer-field/choices ["q-332853/field-1/b4b837c694ca5d8be9308df49d2a8650419e14179be4c20143aec7221e664f88" "q-332853/field-1/517cdf3eae1bd4e0b288721ae6619a788a0f80d4dcce78a511cf52a77482270f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332853" :question/id #uuid "2874f552-32ae-5941-a17c-d4d5a2a61cfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-332853" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+6y=30{e}^{-6x}+4{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $30{e}^{-6x}+4{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{-6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-6x}+\\beta {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-6x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-6x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-6x}-6x{e}^{-6x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x}+\\beta {e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+6(\\alpha x{e}^{-6x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =30{e}^{-6x}+4{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-6x+6x)\\alpha {e}^{-6x}+(\\beta +6\\beta ){e}^{x} & =30{e}^{-6x}+4{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-6x}+7\\beta {e}^{x} & =30{e}^{-6x}+4{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-6x}$ and ${e}^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =30\\,\\text{and}\\,7\\beta =4,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =30\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =\\frac{4}{7}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=30x{e}^{-6x}+\\frac{4}{7}{e}^{x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332853/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c7ce128-42e5-5a5a-8fa2-0e897504ff9d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332853"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332731/field-1/ef4bef4d70288ce15d2f9f2f2b4b7e73def4542a3653160cf524dbbbaca1b399" :answer/id #uuid "00ef8fe8-fc59-5cf6-b756-231f022ea448" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2xe^{6x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332731/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ef1647f0-100d-5b25-9b81-514de8e8f673" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332731/field-1/ef4bef4d70288ce15d2f9f2f2b4b7e73def4542a3653160cf524dbbbaca1b399" :answer-field/choices ["q-332731/field-1/ef4bef4d70288ce15d2f9f2f2b4b7e73def4542a3653160cf524dbbbaca1b399"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332731" :question/id #uuid "dcf09ed4-312b-5c5c-aebe-75b376ad608d" :question/math-academy-id "q-332731" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-6y=2{e}^{6x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{6x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{6x},$ and ${e}^{6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{6x}+6x{e}^{6x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x})-6\\alpha x{e}^{6x} & =2{e}^{6x} \\\\ (1+6x-6x)\\alpha {e}^{6x} & =2{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{6x} & =2{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x{e}^{6x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332731/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c7ce128-42e5-5a5a-8fa2-0e897504ff9d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332731"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332795/field-1/62b058db711e0ac7759151b45e0f61a3c36bb3f31f4846f9df34d1ca5f5d3070" :answer/id #uuid "513b0bbd-042d-5c0a-a8a9-6fa2f0bbc620" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6xe^{-3x}+2e^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332795/field-1/01de3830a7003c3e5dcf8dfff6414d9fba3b9459e106d663be3de9f6591b1894" :answer/id #uuid "45618579-1a6b-55d0-9e61-d7f16d3e0635" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6xe^{-3x}-2e^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332795/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "15624438-d31e-5ef4-a455-9dfaf4cf1ce5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332795/field-1/62b058db711e0ac7759151b45e0f61a3c36bb3f31f4846f9df34d1ca5f5d3070" :answer-field/choices ["q-332795/field-1/62b058db711e0ac7759151b45e0f61a3c36bb3f31f4846f9df34d1ca5f5d3070" "q-332795/field-1/01de3830a7003c3e5dcf8dfff6414d9fba3b9459e106d663be3de9f6591b1894"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332795" :question/id #uuid "0f7c308b-95b8-5ee7-9e1d-2fe096f6d411" :question/math-academy-id "q-332795" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{2x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x})+2\\beta {e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x})+3(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x}) & =6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+(2\\beta +3\\beta ){e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x}+5\\beta {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-3x}$ and ${e}^{2x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =6\\,\\text{and}\\,5\\beta =10,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =6\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{2x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332795/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c7ce128-42e5-5a5a-8fa2-0e897504ff9d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332795"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332730/field-1/5a8ebc3aa4bb665a96d233cdea128d1455ea68c0c10d24c11e2e7290b7d4c7fb" :answer/id #uuid "43e96581-a017-53b3-883d-ab5335a4dd39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8xe^{3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332730/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "bc3dd1ce-9176-54d6-96c0-40e70e1bcbb5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332730/field-1/5a8ebc3aa4bb665a96d233cdea128d1455ea68c0c10d24c11e2e7290b7d4c7fb" :answer-field/choices ["q-332730/field-1/5a8ebc3aa4bb665a96d233cdea128d1455ea68c0c10d24c11e2e7290b7d4c7fb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332730" :question/id #uuid "5d53e2ef-16e9-5372-9be6-95bdefb936d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-332730" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=8{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{3x},$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x})-3\\alpha x{e}^{3x} & =8{e}^{3x} \\\\ (1+3x-3x)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =8{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =8{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{3x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332730/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c7ce128-42e5-5a5a-8fa2-0e897504ff9d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332730"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333179/field-1/bcb2bc406340a9344762903847e186137bc0cd2d67d2c2a9cb8a64624eb0352f" :answer/id #uuid "4f4c664e-62eb-5ba6-a917-29cea6952ac1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Ae^{3x}+4e^{5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333179/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "21bb9839-7c25-55da-ba9e-228cc7a50fda" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333179/field-1/bcb2bc406340a9344762903847e186137bc0cd2d67d2c2a9cb8a64624eb0352f" :answer-field/choices ["q-333179/field-1/bcb2bc406340a9344762903847e186137bc0cd2d67d2c2a9cb8a64624eb0352f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333179" :question/id #uuid "179baab3-20b3-5aca-9b30-ba7d12457edb" :question/math-academy-id "q-333179" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=8{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′}=\\alpha {e}^{5x}\\cdot 5=5\\alpha {e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\alpha {e}^{5x}-3\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+4{e}^{5x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333179/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af1f1fb7-3e26-5624-ac07-0be869d2bd5e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333179"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332895/field-1/3a4d6f777d01d3d90c7e3e5f2f45d70adc69b8e1d5bd67c4fd5695c9f5563efc" :answer/id #uuid "38dede99-f661-51a4-8cb0-0d8ab909dbfd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Ae^{2x}+5xe^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332895/field-1/a96934e1daf18149c9aaeeb5137841e329efb961d6403e3c5aab253622aae4ad" :answer/id #uuid "ef9800da-a179-5890-a4ee-f7b40850a699" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Ae^{2x}+5e^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332895/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5d6be2b9-32ff-58c2-bd7b-c9805bf5f3af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332895/field-1/3a4d6f777d01d3d90c7e3e5f2f45d70adc69b8e1d5bd67c4fd5695c9f5563efc" :answer-field/choices ["q-332895/field-1/3a4d6f777d01d3d90c7e3e5f2f45d70adc69b8e1d5bd67c4fd5695c9f5563efc" "q-332895/field-1/a96934e1daf18149c9aaeeb5137841e329efb961d6403e3c5aab253622aae4ad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332895" :question/id #uuid "30b74f30-6c22-5b7d-8b5b-0cd838e6fa68" :question/math-academy-id "q-332895" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=5{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x})-2\\alpha x{e}^{2x} & =5{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1+2x-2x)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =5{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =5{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+5x{e}^{2x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332895/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af1f1fb7-3e26-5624-ac07-0be869d2bd5e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332895"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332899/field-1/862a2b2d587f5ee6d5770673950a91d9abcee6fd842f8ffe14c4971bc7d65a69" :answer/id #uuid "191a3055-9402-5d37-999e-9b9b581bb9ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Ae^{-2x}+6xe^{-2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332899/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c13c802d-59f6-5ca0-bb6d-c38dbbb3ffcf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332899/field-1/862a2b2d587f5ee6d5770673950a91d9abcee6fd842f8ffe14c4971bc7d65a69" :answer-field/choices ["q-332899/field-1/862a2b2d587f5ee6d5770673950a91d9abcee6fd842f8ffe14c4971bc7d65a69"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332899" :question/id #uuid "125a2d70-6256-5da0-91ae-2caa9110220e" :question/math-academy-id "q-332899" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=6{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{-2x},$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x})+2\\alpha x{e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (1-2x+2x)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+6x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332899/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af1f1fb7-3e26-5624-ac07-0be869d2bd5e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332899"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332898/field-1/bd15ebcb7132e46e407511ad60b4247cd8e3c341d1009a0da9b3537cd19ad990" :answer/id #uuid "ad7e1c04-4c4a-509d-824e-0d678d0629b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Ae^{3x}+4xe^{3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332898/field-1/b80f7fd19ba3033bf76db530484291731add738643598ac5b091902da8240f88" :answer/id #uuid "990cf1be-b2ee-5d1a-93ec-81c6c5845c88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Ae^{3x}+4e^{3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332898/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d4006666-11b2-57a3-9efc-38fe316e73f1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332898/field-1/bd15ebcb7132e46e407511ad60b4247cd8e3c341d1009a0da9b3537cd19ad990" :answer-field/choices ["q-332898/field-1/bd15ebcb7132e46e407511ad60b4247cd8e3c341d1009a0da9b3537cd19ad990" "q-332898/field-1/b80f7fd19ba3033bf76db530484291731add738643598ac5b091902da8240f88"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332898" :question/id #uuid "4446c513-dd4a-5bfc-a354-84536434cfee" :question/math-academy-id "q-332898" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=4{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $4{e}^{3x},$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x})-3\\alpha x{e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ (1+3x-3x)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4x{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+4x{e}^{3x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332898/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af1f1fb7-3e26-5624-ac07-0be869d2bd5e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332898"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333168/field-1/dfb099907e925f7a8f04f971dd9d48ea30988497eea94d5c1e5ae486a14e7ecf" :answer/id #uuid "b1ccd744-7c5b-5dd7-b178-338e537ce94a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Ae^{7x}+5xe^{7x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333168/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7abc8f11-3e28-5ffb-b486-3c9b84c627a0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333168/field-1/dfb09990