ticular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5x{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{7x}+5x{e}^{7x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-333168/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af1f1fb7-3e26-5624-ac07-0be869d2bd5e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333168"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332905/field-1/5ae3fa1194a4f729235b8f64f3764f0dd7bc49f955efcea928fe124c638fd201" :answer/id #uuid "6d2e5db9-c839-5c17-bbd8-91b23bbcefef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-e^{-x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332905/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a19adc36-bc29-58af-80a7-57e720400f46" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332905/field-1/5ae3fa1194a4f729235b8f64f3764f0dd7bc49f955efcea928fe124c638fd201" :answer-field/choices ["q-332905/field-1/5ae3fa1194a4f729235b8f64f3764f0dd7bc49f955efcea928fe124c638fd201"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332905" :question/id #uuid "6831a4d6-1e31-5c67-a7a3-dbfefc73c071" :question/math-academy-id "q-332905" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=5{e}^{-x},\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=5{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{-x},$ and ${e}^{-x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-\\alpha {e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\alpha {e}^{-x})-4(\\alpha {e}^{-x}) & =5{e}^{-x} \\\\ (-1-4)\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =5{e}^{-x} \\\\ -5\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =5{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1 & =-1 \\\\ A & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-{e}^{-x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332905/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332905"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332906/field-1/28dd5a734d70497762e5fbc1e214a5a163f3c2ff4f5630816e6f4a484478f717" :answer/id #uuid "0d72fd22-b3b8-5eca-819b-e8a15a7bcc18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}e^{3x}-\\frac{7}{5}e^{-2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332906/field-1/9dc66d208c5f7f1d463a24409a86c7838633d2fc33a9f267c878343ee668be3b" :answer/id #uuid "3aa5a73b-2fad-5da6-bf98-625c3baa4b0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}e^{3x}+\\frac{7}{5}e^{-2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332906/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7fa6f4a5-e592-5385-829c-d9f968305d6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332906/field-1/28dd5a734d70497762e5fbc1e214a5a163f3c2ff4f5630816e6f4a484478f717" :answer-field/choices ["q-332906/field-1/28dd5a734d70497762e5fbc1e214a5a163f3c2ff4f5630816e6f4a484478f717" "q-332906/field-1/9dc66d208c5f7f1d463a24409a86c7838633d2fc33a9f267c878343ee668be3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332906" :question/id #uuid "6a819ca6-9c6f-5210-9688-e064f0dedcf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-332906" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=7{e}^{-2x},\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=7{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $7{e}^{-2x},$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x}-3\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =7{e}^{-2x} \\\\ -5\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =7{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-\\frac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}-\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\frac{7}{5} & =0 \\\\ A & =\\frac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{3x}-\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332906/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332906"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332903/field-1/2995bb779dff06638655e54a15b221880ad69211a6689dbafc18e0282e96a2f1" :answer/id #uuid "539851df-ec55-5292-9b6c-06c99c429fbd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6e^{5x}-2e^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332903/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "3b87ff12-28b5-5f13-baf9-f8eef8635a1c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332903/field-1/2995bb779dff06638655e54a15b221880ad69211a6689dbafc18e0282e96a2f1" :answer-field/choices ["q-332903/field-1/2995bb779dff06638655e54a15b221880ad69211a6689dbafc18e0282e96a2f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332903" :question/id #uuid "562c468c-2112-5dbe-80f8-72ee844881f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-332903" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=6{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=4\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=6{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\alpha {e}^{2x})-5(\\alpha {e}^{2x}) & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ (2-5)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ -3\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}-2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2 & =4 \\\\ A & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=6{e}^{5x}-2{e}^{2x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332903/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332903"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332909/field-1/68ffd279feb00f429ae030376920451fa7beffd709fce711052fb38806e06847" :answer/id #uuid "1c15459d-03a2-598e-9950-72958fea26ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3e^{-2x}+5e^{-3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332909/field-1/f5efab3003f88a176d8534934a1799de2427c7439aabbb04e682e031b199494b" :answer/id #uuid "e5bc8efc-f29b-54c5-9632-3288541ed307" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3e^{-2x}-5e^{-3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332909/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ff774b99-6268-59bc-b6fa-6cefd366084f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332909/field-1/68ffd279feb00f429ae030376920451fa7beffd709fce711052fb38806e06847" :answer-field/choices ["q-332909/field-1/68ffd279feb00f429ae030376920451fa7beffd709fce711052fb38806e06847" "q-332909/field-1/f5efab3003f88a176d8534934a1799de2427c7439aabbb04e682e031b199494b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332909" :question/id #uuid "9176374c-4880-5679-b51e-bd8fad9c53cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-332909" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=-5{e}^{-3x},\\,y(0)=8\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=-5{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-5{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+2\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =-5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ -\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =-5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=8$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+5 & =8 \\\\ A & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=3{e}^{-2x}+5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332909/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332909"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332936/field-1/4293495940006f0a850418edfcaf499fc6928edcbbf39af73336e5a4b462655c" :answer/id #uuid "5a207f20-012a-5bf8-8e0f-cbd6e4d85cec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-e^{-2x}+8xe^{-2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332936/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "68a0aa56-f1e5-5c50-8e49-dda41327282d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332936/field-1/4293495940006f0a850418edfcaf499fc6928edcbbf39af73336e5a4b462655c" :answer-field/choices ["q-332936/field-1/4293495940006f0a850418edfcaf499fc6928edcbbf39af73336e5a4b462655c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332936" :question/id #uuid "17681023-3db0-5972-8201-5fef0fb45f47" :question/math-academy-id "q-332936" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=8{e}^{-2x},\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=8{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{-2x},$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x})+2(\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}) & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (1-2x+2x)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =-1 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-{e}^{-2x}+8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332936/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332936"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21615"] :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} - 2 y =  e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  A e ^ {2x}$, find the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x},$ and neither $e^{x}$ nor $e^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{x} + \\beta e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha e^{x} + 3\\beta e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha {e}^{x}+3\\beta {e}^{3x})-2(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{3x}) & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\ (\\alpha -2\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(3\\beta -2\\beta ){e}^{3x} & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\ -\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{3x} & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{x}$ and $e^{3x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-\\alpha = 1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = -1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = - e ^{x} - 2 e^{3x}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21621"] :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' + 5y = 15e^{-5x} + 12e^{x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  Ae^{-5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $15 e^{-5x} + 12 e^{x},$ and $e^{-5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while $e^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x  e ^ {-5 x} + \\beta e^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-5x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-5x}-5x{e}^{-5x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+5(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-5x+5x)\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+(\\beta +5\\beta ){e}^{x} & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-5x}+6\\beta {e}^{x} & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{-5x}$ and $e^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 6\\beta = 12,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\beta = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 15x  e ^ {-5x} + 2 e^{x}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21616"] :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' - 6 y = 9 e ^ {6 x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x) = Ae^{6x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 6 y_c=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e^{6x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9 e ^ {6 x},$ and $e^{6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x  e ^ {6x} ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of $y_p$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{6x}+6x{e}^{6x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x})-6\\alpha x{e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ (1+6x-6x)\\alpha {e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 9x  e ^ {6 x} .\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  Ae^{6x} + 9xe^{6x}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21617"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' - 2y = 4e^{3x}, \\qquad y(0) =  1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' - 2y = 4e^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c = Ae^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 2y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $4e^{3x},$ and $e^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\ny_p' = 3\\alpha e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3\\alpha {e}^{3x})-2(\\alpha {e}^{3x}) & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ (3-2)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 4e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{2x} + 4e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) =1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4 & =1 \\\\ A & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -3e^{2x} + 4e ^{3x}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
